<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Decolonisation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Decolonisation refers to the process by which colonies gained independence from imperial powers, especially in the decades after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It brought the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> as global colonial systems, and the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> in 1945 likewise freed its former colonies. The process reshaped the political map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/decolonisation.jpg" alt="Decolonisation" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rjruiziii · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Star_Monument,_Accra,_Ghana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Decolonisation is the ending of colonialism, the process by which imperial nations establish and dominate foreign territories, often overseas. The meanings of the term are disputed. According to David Strang it is achieved through sovereign statehood with recognition by the international community or through full incorporation into an existing sovereign state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> holds that the core requirement is the right to self-determination, which can be exercised with or without independence.</p><p>As a movement for independence from the metropoles, decolonisation began in 1775 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. In the two hundred years after 1783, 165 colonies gained independence from Western imperial powers. Explanations differ: some stress rising education, some economic shifts, some the inspiration of earlier revolutions and some the rising cost of empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first colonies to declare independence were the thirteen North American colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1776. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, a revolt of 1789 and a slave uprising of 1791 in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, led to independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1804. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> cut the links between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its American colonies and began their decolonisation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> became independent from Portugal in 1822.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the German, Austro-Hungarian, Ottoman and Russian empires broke up, and former German and Ottoman territories were governed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandates</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Nationalism in the French colonies strengthened between the wars, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-el-krim">Abd el-Krim</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> of 1921 to 1925 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Lebanon declared independence in 1943 and Syria in 1945.</p><p>After 1945 a wave of decolonisation came first in Asia, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and in the 1960s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>. It was often not carefully planned but a response to politics in the colony, politics at home and international pressure. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> usually held a constitutional conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> before independence. The last wave followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, with the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a> and the handovers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>New states had to build governments, laws, armies and schools, to create loyalty to the state through flags, anthems and official histories, and to develop currencies, banks and taxation. In settler colonial states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, settlers stayed, and Indigenous peoples continue to make demands for decolonisation and self-determination.</p><p>David Strang writes that the loss of their empires turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> into second-rate powers. John Kenneth Galbraith argues that decolonisation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> had economic causes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Decolonization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…gave the dominions full legislative independence, effectively turning the white settler empire into a voluntary Commonwealth well before the main wave of <b>decolonisation</b>, while the Crown Colonies and India were governed under systems that combined administrative efficiency with an increasingly indefensible exclusion of their peoples from political power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The wartime empire's pan-Asian anti-colonial rhetoric, however cynically deployed, and its humiliation of European colonial armies in 1941 and 1942 weakened the legitimacy of European rule in Southeast Asia and contributed to the rapid <b>decolonisation</b> that followed 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…great settler colony, governed for 132 years as part of France on a basis of land seizure and legal inequality; its violent conquest and the war of independence that ended it in 1962 reshaped both modern Algeria and the French Republic and became a defining episode of <b>decolonisation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>French educational institutions of the colonial period produced the post-independence leadership class of much of Francophone Africa, and the close networks between Paris and these elites, known as Françafrique, shaped African politics long after formal <b>decolonisation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Decolonisation</b> was abrupt: the Congo became independent on 30 June 1960 and descended into crisis, and Rwanda and Burundi followed in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Nehru India was a leader of <b>decolonisation</b> and, with Yugoslavia, Egypt and Indonesia, of the Non-Aligned Movement founded at Belgrade in 1961, and Indian troops served widely in United Nations peacekeeping missions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
